      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients. Specifically, if a polynomial has rational coefficients, then for any irrational root \\(a + \\sqrt{b}\\), the conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\) must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nGiven the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), the conjugates \\(1-\\sqrt{2}, 2-\\sqrt{3}, 3-\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\) must also be roots of the polynomial. Therefore, we have 1000 pairs of roots, leading to a total of \\(1000 \\times 2 = 2000\\) roots.\n\nSince the original polynomial already has 1000 roots (the original numbers), and we need to consider the conjugates, the smallest possible degree of the polynomial is \\(1000 + 2000 = 3000\\).\n\nLet's confirm this reasoning with a simple Python code that calculates the total number of roots:\n```python\n# Number of original roots\noriginal_roots = 1000\n\n# Number of conjugates needed (each original root has a conjugate)\nconjugates_needed = original_roots * 2\n\n# Total number of roots for the polynomial\ntotal_roots = original_roots + conjugates_needed\nprint(total_roots)\n```\n```output\n3000\n```\nThe smallest possible degree of the polynomial with rational coefficients that has all of the numbers \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) as roots is \\(\\boxed{3000}\\).",
