      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the properties of polynomials with rational coefficients and how to construct a polynomial with given roots. \n\nGiven that the polynomial has rational coefficients and the roots \\(1+\\sqrt{2}, 2+\\sqrt{3}, 3+\\sqrt{4}, \\dots, 1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\), it's important to note that if a polynomial with rational coefficients has a root, say \\(a+\\sqrt{b}\\), then its conjugate, \\(a-\\sqrt{b}\\), must also be a root of the polynomial.\n\nThus, if \\(k+1+\\sqrt{k+2}\\) is a root, then \\(k+1-\\sqrt{k+2}\\) must also be a root. \n\nWe need to determine the total number of roots and how this translates into the degree of the polynomial. Each of the numbers from \\(1\\) to \\(1000\\) will be followed by its negative counterpart when \\(k\\) is an integer from \\(1\\) to \\(1000\\). This means, each of the numbers \\(1, 2, 3, \\dots, 1000\\) has two corresponding roots: \\(k+1+\\sqrt{k+2}\\) and \\(k+1-\\sqrt{k+2}\\). \n\nSince there are 1000 pairs of these roots, the total number of distinct irrational roots is \\(2 \\times 1000 = 2000\\). However, since we assumed that only the highest root \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) was given (and not both \\(1000+\\sqrt{1001}\\) and \\(1000-\\sqrt{1001}\\)), the total number of roots is \\(2000\\).\n\nThis means that any polynomial with these roots must be of degree at least \\(2000\\), since having more than 2000 distinct roots is necessary to accommodate all such roots (both positive and their negatives). Any polynomial with fewer than 2000 roots could not accommodate all of these roots.\n\nThus, the smallest possible degree of such a polynomial is \\(2000\\).\n\nThe final answer is therefore \\( \\boxed{2000} \\).",
